memory: mtk-smi: give mt6797 its own smi-common data, not mt6795's

memory/0001 pointed the mt6797 smi-common compatible at
mtk_smi_common_mt6795, on the same "modelled on mt6795" assumption that
memory/0003 has already had to undo once for the larb's SMI_LARB_MMU_EN
offset. The assumption is wrong here too, and in two ways that matter
exactly when the display is first put behind the M4U -- which is the
first time this device ever runtime-resumes smi-common, and therefore
the first time any of mt6795's values are written to hardware at all.

The vendor SMI driver for this exact SoC (CONFIG_ARCH_MT6797 builds
drivers/misc/mediatek/smi with -DSMI_EV) contradicts mt6795 on both
fields it carries:

1. SMI_BUS_SEL. smi_configuration.c's smi_profile_setting_common_init
   writes 0x220 = 0x1554, i.e. F_MMU1_LARB(1..6): larb0 to MMU0 and
   every other larb to MMU1. mt6795 asks for F_MMU1_LARB(0) = 0x1,
   which is the opposite for larb0 specifically. larb0 is the display
   and the only larb this tree instantiates, so for us 0x1554 and the
   mainline default of 0 are the same thing and 0x1 is the one value
   that is not.

2. The init table's offsets. mt6795 uses the layout this file calls
   SMI_L1_ARB (0x200). mt6797 does not have it: smi_reg.h puts
   SMI_L1LEN at 0x100 and L1ARB0..L1ARB6 at 0x104..0x11c, and 0x200 is
   not in the SoC's smi-common register map at all -- it is absent from
   the vendor's own smi_common_debug_offset dump list. So the inherited
   table writes 0x1b to something that is not L1_ARB on this chip while
   leaving all seven real L1ARB registers at reset.

The vendor's full mt6797 values are transcribed in a comment and
deliberately not applied. Verified with devmem on kernel #34, with the
desktop up and the panel lit: 0x14022100..0x1402211c, 0x220, 0x230,
0x234, 0x238 and 0x300 all read 0x00000000. They have never been
written on this machine and the display works. They are arbitration and
FIFO tuning rather than correctness, and landing twelve unmeasured
register writes in the same kernel that first makes the display
translate would make the result uninterpretable either way. If
bandwidth or underruns show up behind the M4U, that comment is where to
start.

Relevant to B-44: on #36 the display stalled with flip_done timeouts,
OVL_SRC_CON = 0 and zero IOMMU faults. Three things changed at once in
that kernel -- the ports began translating, larb0 was runtime-resumed
for the first time, and smi-common was configured for the first time,
with these values. This patch removes the third from the list.
--- a/drivers/memory/mtk-smi.c
+++ b/drivers/memory/mtk-smi.c
@@ -699,6 +699,59 @@
 	.init     = mtk_smi_common_mt6795_init,
 };
 
+/*
+ * MT6797 (Helio X20). It was pointed at mtk_smi_common_mt6795 on the same
+ * "modelled on mt6795" assumption that put the larb's MMU-enable bits at the
+ * wrong address for months, and it is wrong here too. The vendor SMI driver
+ * for this exact SoC (CONFIG_ARCH_MT6797 builds drivers/misc/mediatek/smi with
+ * -DSMI_EV) disagrees with mt6795 on both fields:
+ *
+ * 1. bus_sel. smi_configuration.c's smi_profile_setting_common_init writes
+ *    SMI_BUS_SEL (0x220) = 0x1554 = F_MMU1_LARB(1..6) -- larb0 goes to MMU0
+ *    and everything else to MMU1. MT6795 asks for the exact opposite for
+ *    larb0 (F_MMU1_LARB(0) = 0x1). larb0 is the display, and it is the only
+ *    larb this tree instantiates, so 0 and 0x1554 are behaviourally identical
+ *    for us; 0x1 is the one value that is not. Leaving bus_sel at the default
+ *    0 is therefore both the mainline default and what the vendor asks for
+ *    where it can be observed.
+ *
+ * 2. The init table offsets. MT6795 uses the register layout this file calls
+ *    SMI_L1_ARB (0x200). MT6797 does not have that layout: smi_reg.h puts
+ *    SMI_L1LEN at 0x100 and L1ARB0..L1ARB6 at 0x104..0x11c (the non-SMI_R
+ *    branch), and 0x200 does not appear in the SoC's SMI common register map
+ *    at all. So mtk_smi_common_mt6795_init writes 0x1b into a register that
+ *    is not L1_ARB on this chip, and leaves every real L1ARB at reset.
+ *
+ * The vendor's own values, for whoever needs them (all offsets from
+ * drivers/misc/mediatek/smi/smi_reg.h, values from smi_configuration.c):
+ *
+ *      0x100 SMI_L1LEN        0xb
+ *      0x104 SMI_L1ARB0       0x18fc     0x108 L1ARB1  0x1000
+ *      0x10c SMI_L1ARB2       0x1000     0x110 L1ARB3  0x1000
+ *      0x114 SMI_L1ARB4       0x1000     0x118 L1ARB5  0x126a
+ *      0x11c SMI_L1ARB6       0x1000
+ *      0x220 SMI_BUS_SEL      0x1554
+ *      0x230 SMI_READ_FIFO_TH 0x747f
+ *      0x234 SMI_M4U_TH       0xf7e19085
+ *      0x238 SMI_FIFO_TH1     0x43214e8
+ *      0x300 SMI_DCM          0x403
+ *
+ * They are deliberately NOT applied here. Until the display was put behind
+ * the M4U this device never runtime-resumed smi-common at all, so every one
+ * of those registers is still at its reset value on a running desktop
+ * (verified with devmem on kernel #34: 0x14022100..0x1402211c, 0x220, 0x230,
+ * 0x234, 0x238 and 0x300 all read 0x00000000) -- and the panel works. They
+ * are arbitration and FIFO tuning, not correctness, and applying twelve
+ * unmeasured register writes in the same change that first makes the display
+ * translate is how you end up unable to say which one mattered. If bandwidth
+ * or underruns become a problem behind the IOMMU, start here.
+ */
+static const struct mtk_smi_common_plat mtk_smi_common_mt6797 = {
+	.type	  = MTK_SMI_GEN2,
+	/* bus_sel 0 (all larbs to MMU0) -- see above; NOT F_MMU1_LARB(0). */
+	/* .init unset -- mt6795's table addresses registers this SoC lacks. */
+};
+
 static const struct mtk_smi_common_plat mtk_smi_common_mt8183 = {
 	.type     = MTK_SMI_GEN2,
 	.has_gals = true,
@@ -761,7 +814,7 @@
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt2712-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_gen2},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6779-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_mt6779},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6795-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_mt6795},
-	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_mt6795},
+	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_mt6797},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt8167-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_gen2},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt8173-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_gen2},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt8183-smi-common", .data = &mtk_smi_common_mt8183},
